@doc """
Kernel module.

Provides the small auto-imported standard surface for cross-cutting builtins
and special forms.
The names if, if_then, require, and ensure are ordinary names; their Lazy
argument rules apply only when these standard declarations are selected.
and / or and the Pattern consumer names are reserved.
Use this layer for operations that are not owned by a single primitive module
such as conditional special forms, printing, debug inspection, sequence
decomposition, and process-oriented entrypoint control.

## REPL
xldr(1)> "hello"
hello
xldr(2)> ("alice", 42)
("alice", 42)
"""
@autoimport
defmod Kernel {
  @doc """
  Construct `Unit` in an `Alternative` context when `condition` is true,
  otherwise return that context's empty value.

  The context can be selected explicitly (`guard::<Option>(True)`) or inferred
  from the expected result type (`value: Option<Unit> = guard(True)`).
  """
  def guard::<Alternative>(condition: Boolean) -> Alternative<Unit> {
    if(condition, Applicative::pure(()), Alternative::empty())
  }

  @doc """
  Conditional execution for side-effect-oriented code paths.

  The declared type is:
  `if_then(Boolean, Lazy<Unit>) -> Unit`

  `Lazy<T>` is a compiler marker declared by standard APIs to express
  delayed evaluation. This does not mean everyday source code must wrap
  the argument in an explicit closure.

  Ordinary expression form stays available:
  `if_then(flag, print("flag is true"))`

  The compiler normalizes the branch to a zero-argument callable and invokes
  it once only when selected. A directly written call is wrapped once;
  a parenthesized expression is evaluated first, before normalization.
  Capturing a branch placeholder requires `(-> Unit)` rather than `Unit`.
  `&if_then(&1, &2)` has type `(Boolean, (-> Unit) -> Unit)`.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> if_then(True, print("flag is true"))
  flag is true
  xldr(2)> when_true = &if_then(&1, &2)
  xldr(3)> when_true(True, {|| print("selected")})
  selected
  """
  @builtin def if_then(flag: Boolean, then_branch: Lazy<Unit>) -> Unit

  @doc """
  Branching expression that returns one value from two alternatives.

  The declared type is:
  `if(Boolean, Lazy<$A>, Lazy<$A>) -> $A`

  Both branches are marked as `Lazy<_>` because Surtr treats `if` as a
  language-level special form.
  Only the selected branch is evaluated, and both branches must agree on the
  same result type.

  This is a semantic contract, not a requirement that user code spell the
  branches as explicit closures.
  Plain expression style is valid and is the normal way to write code:

  `if(flag, "ok", err_reason)`

  At a `Lazy<_>` argument, parentheses are an eager boundary. For example,
  `if(flag, selected(), (prepare()))` evaluates `prepare()` once before the
  condition selects a branch; without parentheses it evaluates only when the
  branch is selected.

  Normalization invokes the common branch once. A remaining callable is
  returned as a value, without an additional invocation. The known branch
  determines the normalized type of a captured branch placeholder; an outer
  annotation cannot change that normalization or evaluation behavior.

  A branch placeholder in a capture receives the normalized zero-argument
  callable type. `&N` and `(&N)` have the same meaning in that position.
  A fixed eager expression `(EXPR)` reads the local context before capture
  parameters are introduced and cannot refer to a capture placeholder.
  For example, `&if(&1, (1 + &2), 0)` is rejected. Fixed eager expressions
  still run once before branch selection on each invocation of the capture.
  If both branches are placeholders, an expected callable type or annotation
  must determine their signature. For example, `&if(&1, &2, 0)` has type
  `(Boolean, (-> Int) -> Int)`; its second argument is a callable, not an `Int`.

  When the branches both return `Unit`, prefer `if_then(...)` to make the
  intent explicit.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> if(True, "ok", "ng")
  "ok"
  xldr(2)> choose = &if(&1, &2, 0)
  xldr(3)> choose(True, {|| 42})
  42
  """
  @builtin def if(flag: Boolean, then_branch: Lazy<$A>, else_branch: Lazy<$A>) -> $A

  @doc """
  Pattern-aware branching expression for a single success path.

  The declared type is:
  `if_let($A, $Pattern, Lazy<$B>, Lazy<$B>) -> $B`

  `if_let` evaluates the input value once, checks it against the pattern, and:
  - evaluates `then_branch` when the pattern matches
  - evaluates `else_branch` when it does not match

  Any bindings introduced by the pattern are available only inside the
  successful branch body. A binding-producing pattern requires a directly
  written success expression, even when the expression does not use a binding.
  Without bindings, the success branch follows ordinary Lazy normalization.
  A parenthesized eager expression resolves names before pattern matching,
  so it cannot use a binding introduced by this pattern. In a captured call,
  it also cannot refer to a capture placeholder: `x + &1` is a direct
  success expression, while `(x + &1)` is rejected even if an outer `x` exists.
  A direct placeholder `&N` or `(&N)` is not an eager expression.

  A complete call can be captured with the pattern written in place, such as
  `&if_let(&1, Ok(x), x + &2, 0)`. Here `&2` receives ordinary `Int` data.
  The generated function may be passed through ordinary parameters, returns,
  and variables; each invocation binds the pattern names only in its own
  successful branch. A closure created in that branch may retain the bound
  values according to the ordinary lexical-capture rules. A function value
  bound by the Pattern itself remains ordinary data in the success scope;
  this is distinct from replacing the branch with an external thunk.
  Pattern pre-argument expressions, projection slots `_1` through `_16`, and
  capture placeholders `&1` through `&16` retain their separate roles.
  The pattern itself cannot be replaced by a placeholder. With no bindings,
  `&if_let(Ok(1), Ok(_), &1, 0)` has type `((-> Int) -> Int)`.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> if_let(Ok(1), Ok(value), to_string(value), "miss")
  "1"
  xldr(2)> on_ok = &if_let(Ok(1), Ok(_), &1, 0)
  xldr(3)> on_ok({|| 42})
  42
  xldr(4)> add_on_ok = &if_let(&1, Ok(x), x + &2, 0)
  xldr(5)> add_on_ok(Ok(1), 2)
  3
  xldr(6)> outer_value = 10
  xldr(7)> fixed = &if_let(&1, Ok(outer_value), (outer_value + 1), 0)
  xldr(8)> fixed(Ok(99))
  11
  """
  @builtin def if_let(value: $A, pattern: $Pattern, then_branch: Lazy<$B>, else_branch: Lazy<$B>) -> $B

  @doc """
  Pattern-aware side-effect guard for a single success path.

  The declared type is:
  `if_let_then($A, $Pattern, Lazy<Unit>) -> Unit`

  The input value is evaluated once.
  The branch expression is evaluated only when the pattern matches.
  Non-match returns `Unit` without evaluating the branch expression.
  A binding-producing pattern requires a directly written success expression;
  a binding-free pattern uses ordinary Lazy normalization. Eager expressions
  resolve names before matching and cannot refer to new pattern bindings.
  In a captured call they also cannot refer to capture placeholders.
  Direct placeholders `&N` and `(&N)` remain ordinary parameter references.
  Complete calls can be captured with a fixed pattern. Bare consumer captures
  and placeholders replacing the pattern itself are rejected.
  `&if_let_then(Ok(1), Ok(_), &1)` has type `((-> Unit) -> Unit)`.
  A binding-producing pattern instead keeps the success expression in the
  matching scope; its data placeholders are not converted to thunks.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> if_let_then(Ok(1), Ok(value), print(to_string(value)))
  1
  xldr(2)> on_match = &if_let_then(Ok(1), Ok(_), &1)
  xldr(3)> on_match({|| print("matched")})
  matched
  """
  @builtin def if_let_then(value: $A, pattern: $Pattern, then_branch: Lazy<Unit>) -> Unit

  @doc """
  Boolean predicate for pattern compatibility.

  The declared type is:
  `is_match($A, $Pattern) -> Boolean`

  Returns `True` when the value matches the pattern, otherwise `False`.
  This form does not permit binding variables in the pattern argument.
  Prefix, Kernel-qualified, backtick-prefix, and infix calls use the same
  Pattern grammar. An infix call with `is_match` accepts an OR Pattern.
  Use `_` or an identifier beginning with `_` to ignore values, or switch to `if_let` / `match` when bindings
  are required.
  A complete call such as `&is_match(&1, Ok(_))` can be captured.
  The pattern must be written directly; bare capture and replacing the pattern
  with a placeholder are rejected.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> is_match(Ok(1), Ok(_))
  True
  """
  @builtin def is_match(value: $A, pattern: $Pattern) -> Boolean

  @doc """
  Match the whole input and return the selected values in a Result.

  Projection slots `_1` through `_16` belong to this Pattern only. Slots must
  be unique and consecutive from `_1`; their numbers determine output order.
  Zero slots return Unit, one returns its value, and multiple slots return a tuple.
  Extractor failures preserve their original Error. Failure returns here without
  returning from the enclosing function. Result inputs are not unwrapped.
  Complete calls such as `&apply_pattern(&1, [_1, .._])` can be captured.
  Projection slots belong to the pattern, while `&1` is a capture argument.
  The pattern itself cannot be replaced by a capture placeholder.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> apply_pattern([10, 20], [_1: Int, .._2: List<Int>])
  Ok((10, [20]))
  xldr(2)> apply_pattern(Ok(3), Ok(_1))
  Ok(3)
  xldr(3)> [10, 20] |> apply_pattern([_, _1])
  Ok(20)
  """
  @builtin def apply_pattern(value: $Value, pattern: $Pattern) -> Result<$Return>


  @doc """
  Duplicate one value into a pair.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> dup(7)
  (7, 7)
  """
  def dup(value: $A) -> ($A, $A) { (value, value) }

  @doc """
  Logical conjunction with short-circuit evaluation.

  The declared type is:
  `and(Boolean, Lazy<Boolean>) -> Boolean`

  Even though the surface contract looks like an ordinary 2-argument function,
  the compiler evaluates the right-hand side only when the left-hand side is
  `True`.
  This keeps call-style source syntax while preserving short-circuit behavior.
  Capturing `&and(&1, &2)` produces `(Boolean, (-> Boolean) -> Boolean)`.
  Call that function with a zero-argument right-hand side, such as
  `both(True, {|| False})`; passing `False` directly is a type error.
  Reusing `&1` for both positions is rejected because their required types differ.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> and(True, False)
  False
  xldr(2)> both = &and(&1, &2)
  xldr(3)> both(True, {|| False})
  False
  """
  @builtin def and(left: Boolean, right: Lazy<Boolean>) -> Boolean

  @doc """
  Logical disjunction with short-circuit evaluation.

  The declared type is:
  `or(Boolean, Lazy<Boolean>) -> Boolean`

  The compiler evaluates the right-hand side only when the left-hand side is
  `False`.
  Like `and`, this is exposed as ordinary call-style syntax while keeping
  short-circuit semantics.
  Capturing `&or(&1, &2)` produces `(Boolean, (-> Boolean) -> Boolean)`.
  Call that function with a zero-argument right-hand side, such as
  `either(False, {|| True})`; its body runs only when the left side is `False`.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> or(False, True)
  True
  xldr(2)> either = &or(&1, &2)
  xldr(3)> either(False, {|| True})
  True
  """
  @builtin def or(left: Boolean, right: Lazy<Boolean>) -> Boolean

  @doc """
  Guard a precondition and return `Result<Unit>`.

  The declared type is:
  `require(Boolean, Lazy<Error>) -> Result<Unit>`

  Like `if`, this is a special form. The error branch is evaluated only when
  the condition is `False`, so success does not pay to build diagnostics.
  Call sites may construct a fresh `deferror` or forward an existing `Error`
  value obtained elsewhere. The returned Err preserves the supplied Error, including
  its kind, message, origin, cause, and call history.

  Parenthesizing the error expression evaluates it once before the condition
  is checked. An error placeholder normalizes to `(-> Error)`, giving
  `&require(&1, &2)` the shape `(Boolean, (-> Error) -> Result<Unit>)`.
  That capture is an ordinary function value; pass a zero-argument Error
  callable for the placeholder. The error may also be fixed in the capture:
  `&require(&1, NoneError())` has type `(Boolean -> Result<Unit>)`.

  Ordinary expression form stays available:
  `require(user.age >= 0, InvalidUserAge(user.age))`

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> require(True, NoneError)
  Ok(())
  xldr(2)> check = &require(&1, NoneError())
  xldr(3)> check(True)
  Ok(())
  """
  @builtin def require(flag: Boolean, err: Lazy<Error>) -> Result<Unit>

  @doc """
  Validate a value and return it unchanged on success.

  The declared type is:
  `ensure($A, ($A -> Boolean), Lazy<Error>) -> Result<$A>`

  The first argument is evaluated once, passed to the predicate, and returned
  inside `Ok(...)` when the predicate succeeds.
  The error branch is evaluated only when validation fails. Call sites may
  construct a fresh `deferror` or forward an existing `Error` value.
  A captured error placeholder normalizes to `(-> Error)`; the predicate
  remains an ordinary unary callable. Both callables can be stored and passed
  as ordinary arguments. The error expression can also be fixed: for an `Int` input,
  `&ensure(&1, {|n| n > 0}, NoneError())` has type `(Int -> Result<Int>)`.

  The predicate must be a unary callable value such as a capture or closure:
  `ensure(num, &is_even, SomeError)` or `ensure(num, {|x| x > 0 }, SomeError)`.
  Call expressions such as `ensure(num, is_even(), SomeError)` are rejected.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> ensure(3, {|n| n > 0}, NoneError)
  Ok(3)
  xldr(2)> positive: (Int -> Result<Int>) = &ensure(&1, {|n| n > 0}, NoneError())
  xldr(3)> positive(3)
  Ok(3)
  """
  @builtin def ensure(value: $A, pred: ($A -> Boolean), err: Lazy<Error>) -> Result<$A>

  @doc """
  Observe `value` for side effects and return it unchanged.
  This is useful in pipelines for logging or metrics without changing the data.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> tap(3, {|n| print(to_string(n))})
  3
  3
  """
  def tap(value: $A, f: ($A -> Unit)) -> $A {
    f(value)
    value
  }

  @doc """
  Decompose a non-empty sequence in MatchBlock positions.

  `uncons(term)` is a builtin Extractor used by `=?` and `match`.
  It accepts both `List` and `String`.

  - `List<$A>` becomes `(head: $A, tail: List<$A>)`
  - `String` becomes `(head: String, tail: String)`

  These are two concrete static contracts selected from the matched input.
  `uncons` does not expose a bare generic target or a Union. The declaration
  below records the List contract; the compiler-owned String pattern route uses
  the String contract while sharing the same runtime primitive.

  In pattern position, `[head, ..tail]` expresses the same head/tail decomposition.
  A structural List pattern has its own failure Error: empty head/tail input
  gives `EmptyList`, whereas direct `uncons(head, tail)` gives `PatternMismatch`.
  Expression position keeps list construction semantics, so `["t", ..source]`
  is still a list expression.

  Empty input to `uncons` returns `MatchResult::Err` with
  `PatternMismatch("Pattern did not match.")`.
  Branch consumers treat this as no match; `=?` preserves the Error.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> match [1, 2] { [head, ..tail] => to_string(head), [] => "empty", }
  "1"
  """
  @builtin defextractor uncons(term: List<$A>) -> MatchResult<($A, List<$A>), Error>

  @doc """
  Print a string to stdout.
  Use `to_string(...)` first when the value is not already `String`.
  This builtin belongs under `Kernel` because printing is part of the shared
  execution surface rather than a helper owned by one primitive type.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> print("hello")
  hello
  """
  @builtin def print(a: String) -> Unit

  @doc """
  Return a string representation for inspecting the structure of a value.
  Strings are quoted, with control characters and interpolation openers escaped.
  A quoted standalone String reproduces the original value when entered as a regular string literal.
  LF and TAB appear as `\n` and `\t`; other control characters, such as ESC, appear as `\u{1b}`.
  Strings passed directly to `print` are not escaped.
  Structs display the names and values of all fields, including private fields, in definition order.
  Nested structs follow the same rules, regardless of the caller's scope or whether Show is implemented.
  Private visibility does not guarantee secrecy, and the display does not grant permission to access or update fields.
  Struct construction and Error displays differ from string literals, so re-entering the full inspect output is not guaranteed to reproduce the value.
  to_string follows the Show contract and does not implicitly substitute inspect.
  Bare callables display their module, name, and signature when runtime metadata is available.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> ("alice", 42)
  ("alice", 42)
  xldr(2)> print(inspect("\u{1b}a"))
  "\u{1b}a"
  """
  @builtin def inspect(a: $A) -> String

  @doc """
  Mark an unimplemented function that returns `Result` by returning `Err(NotImplemented)`.

  ## Examples
  def load_config() -> Result<String> {
    todo()
  }

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> inspect(todo())
  "Err(NotImplemented(\"not implemented\"))"
  """
  def todo::<$T>() -> Result<$T> {
    Err(NotImplemented)
  }

  @doc """
  Print an `Error` value to stderr-oriented output.
  This stays under `Kernel` because it is part of the cross-cutting runtime
  surface, not an `Error`-specific helper family.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> eprint(NoneError)
  Error: NoneError
  """
  @builtin def eprint(err: Error) -> Unit

  @doc """
  Request a process exit code for the current entrypoint execution.
  This is effectful and compile-unit aware, so it stays in the `Kernel`
  surface instead of a primitive-type module.

  Script source may call it directly. Project compilation permits it only in
  the configured entrypoint; definition checks and REPL chunks reject it.

  ## Source
  `set_exit_code(7)`
  """
  @builtin def set_exit_code(code: Int) -> Unit

  @doc """
  Return CLI arguments passed after `--` in `surtr run`.
  This allows zero-argument `main()` entrypoints to read runtime arguments
  through module-level helpers.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> project_args()
  []
  """
  @builtin def project_args() -> List<String>
}
